blockd/tests/commit_cost.rs, release build, aarch64 Linux container, median of 3
per point. One 64 KiB page dirtied per chunk, i.e. the shape a 4 KiB random-write
Guest workload produces, where a tiny write dirties a whole 4 MiB chunk.

Serial commit (copy and hash one chunk at a time):

dirty chunks   pack MiB     flush ms     ms per MiB     sha256 MiB/s
           8         32        131.8           4.12              548
          16         64        266.7           4.17              541
          32        128        491.7           3.84              546
          48        192        749.5           3.90              545

Parallel commit (copy inside the barrier and hash after it, up to 8 threads;
single-chunk commits stay inline):

dirty chunks   pack MiB     flush ms     ms per MiB     sha256 MiB/s
           1          4         22.1           5.53              548
           8         32         80.3           2.51              548
          16         64        156.4           2.44              548
          32        128        269.1           2.10              546
          48        192        411.3           2.14              545

Reading: FLUSH cost is strictly linear in pack bytes, and sha256 at 546 MiB/s per
core is ~1.83 of the ~3.9 ms per MiB, i.e. about 47% of a commit. Parallelising
the copy and the hash gives 1.64x-1.83x on commits of 32-192 MiB.
